From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 21:36:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 18/24] layout: render <use>

Servo's SVGUseElement is a bare DOM stub with no shadow instancing, so
there is nothing under a <use> to walk and it rendered nothing. Earlier
in this series that was recorded as blocked on an id lookup layout does
not have. It is no longer blocked: 0017 built exactly that lookup to
resolve paint servers, and <use> reuses it.

Renders the referenced element as though it were a child of the <use>,
wrapped in the use's own transform composed with translate(x, y).
<symbol> is walked directly, since it only ever renders through a <use>
and build_node otherwise refuses it.

Two deliberate departures from the spec, both recorded in the code. The
referenced element keeps the computed style it has where it is defined
rather than where it is used, so inheritance through the <use> --
most visibly fill="currentColor" on the <use> itself -- does not reach
it; doing that properly means styling the subtree a second time in the
use's inherited context, which is a shadow tree Servo does not build.
And a viewBox on a <symbol> is ignored, since it is treated as a plain
group.

A <use> can reference a group containing another <use>, and nothing
stops that being circular, so the walk is depth-limited and a direct
self-reference is rejected outright. Both are tested: the cycle case
exits cleanly rather than hanging layout.

Validated: six cases -- plain, transformed, group, <symbol>, dangling
reference and self-reference -- render within 0.022% of pixels of the
rasterization path, all antialiasing. svg/ WPT +212 subtests against the
pref-off baseline with zero regressions, up from +199; five use/symbol
reftests flip to passing, including use-symbol-rendered-001 and
use-hidden-attr-change.
